Date: 24 NOV 1980 1647-EST
From: carlf@mit-ai
Sent-by: CARLF at MIT-AI
Subject:  Parallel processors
To: otto at WHARTON-10
CC: SETNET at MIT-AI

   Some of us here are working the design of a 1,000,000 processor system. I'll reply to
your points individually:
  * Each computer can run its own program with its own modest memory
    (1-4Kbytes?)
   Our processors (usually called "cells" around here) have about 0.04 Kbytes each, or about 
150 bits. They don't really have program memory and can only select among microcode streams.
Each is analogous to a cons-cell in Lisp. They only have pointers to 3-4 other cells,
and perhaps store a number or two. When we put lots of them together, however, they will
execute huge programs amazingly fast (we hope).
  * Each computer is ostensibly independent of the rest, but can
    be "acquired" by another to run subtasks
   Were one of these independent, it would be quite useless. They can be tied together,with 
pointers, into trees and other data structures, which can execute parallel operations.  A 
program can be duplicated many times and sent out to a thousand cells for parallel execution.
A structure can be extended by taking over cells which are not being otherwise used.
  * Each computer can send messages to any other computer in the set
    with low efficiency   
  * Each computer can send messages to selected other computers in the  
    set with high efficiency (dynamic establishment of priority links?)
   The cells are arranged in a square grid, with each connected to its 4 nearest nieghbors.
Comunications over longer distances are handled by passing messages from one processor to the 
next. There are no preferred routes. The messages float in the general direction of the 
destination. Of course, the messages which have longer to go take longer, so two cells 
which have a lot to say to each other can transmit themselves through the net until they are 
nieghbors. 
  That's our project, in abbreviated form. Carl Hewitt (carl@mit-ai) is working on something 
which might be more up your alley. You ought to get in touch with him, if you haven't already.

					You're welcome,

						Carl Feynman (carlf@mit-ai)
						speaking for setnet@mit-ai

