danny@MIT-AI 06/27/80 17:59:23
To: SETNET at MIT-AI

	Sam Brown from Texas Instrument visited today. They
are interested cooperating with us as follows:

	Stage I - Simulation of the machine on a large number
of mask programmed micro-processors (probably tms1000). We write
the code. They provide assembler, simulator, and emulator if really want one.
They burn make the mask, burn a few hundered and wire them together into an array.

	Stage II - Waffer scale version of above TI does all the work.

	Stage III- (pipelined with stage II) They make a custom waffer scale
chip to our specifications. They worry about packaging it. 

	Stage IV- They, we or both of us build a machine with about 1,000,000
cells. We (MIT-AI) program it do do some usefull things of our choosing.

	The understanding, should we decide to accept it, would be informal and
non binding. There would be no exchange of confidential information. Each
side would be free to pursue any othert effort in this area without informing
the other. In particular, it is understood that we will probably make our own test
chip with HP.

	Should we agree to this?



